                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Read the Portfolio`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `You inherit the 180-account book: revenue, gross margin, an estimated cost-to-serve, growth potential and how the 6 account managers spend their time. The trap is reading it by <strong>revenue size</strong> — the lens that makes the EUR 13M anchor look untouchable and hides the truth. <em>Revenue is not profit.</em> Build a cost-to-serve view and the picture inverts: the <strong>40 small accounts lose ≈€1.1M a year</strong>, <strong>22 high-margin mid-tier accounts (€9.5M) are starving</strong>, and <strong>55% of KAM time is trapped on the top 3</strong>. Classify by value <em>and</em> potential, then write the one-line diagnosis of where effort is mis-spent.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Allocate Effort & Set Service Levels`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Redesign the commercial model inside the <strong>€1.5M</strong> budget. First, reallocate the 6 account managers' time across segments — how much to defend the anchor, how much to <em>grow</em> the mid-tier, and how much to <em>withdraw</em> from the unprofitable small accounts. Then design differentiated service tiers: move the 40 small accounts to a low-cost <strong>self-serve</strong> tier to recover the €1.1M leak, and fund a real <strong>growth tier</strong> for the high-potential mid-tier. ‘Equal’ service for all 180 accounts is not fair — it is unmanaged cost.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Choose the AI Tool & Defend the Anchor`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Two decisions collide. Deploy <strong>one</strong> AI module well — the data only supports one this quarter, and each needs <strong>€90k of cleanup</strong>; switching on all three gives you three models you trust none of. Then answer Aérospatiale's ultimatum: <strong>9% cut + a dedicated engineer</strong>, or it dual-sources 40% (≈€5.2M at risk). A discount has to <em>buy</em> something — a volume commitment, a longer contract. Caving across the book vaporises ≈€2M of margin for a maybe. And to replace at-risk volume, remember you hold only ~35% of wallet at your top accounts: <em>grow inside the relationships you already have</em> before chasing cold logos.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Recover & Set the Operating Rhythm`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Present to the commercial director and the Aérospatiale buyer. Report the four KPIs — <strong>profitability, retention, share-of-wallet, cost-to-serve</strong> — against the Round 1 baseline, and defend the trade-offs: what you grew, defended and <em>deliberately under-served</em>. Set a credible, reachable share-of-wallet target you can hit via cross-sell — a heroic 70% with no plan does not land. Lock in a <strong>quarterly value-and-potential review</strong> with explicit tier rules and AI usage so the gains stick. Name the account you will consciously under-serve, and hold the line.`,

                    r1_lens_label:          `What lens do you use to read the 180-account book?`,
                    r1_cts_label:           `Do you build a cost-to-serve view before allocating any effort?`,
                    r1_diagnosis_label:     `Write the one-line diagnosis of where effort is mis-spent.`,
                    lens_valuepotential:    `Value & potential — segment on true profitability (margin minus cost-to-serve) AND growth/share-of-wallet headroom`,
                    lens_margin:            `Gross margin only — rank accounts by the margin they book today`,
                    lens_revenue:           `Revenue size — defend the biggest accounts; they are clearly where the business is`,
                    cts_full:               `Yes — estimate cost-to-serve across all segments so the 40 loss-making small accounts become visible`,
                    cts_toponly:            `Partly — estimate it for the top 10 accounts only and move on`,
                    cts_skip:               `No — revenue per account is enough; cost-to-serve is an accounting nicety`,
                    diag_trapped:           `55% of KAM time is trapped on the top 3 while 22 high-margin mid-tier accounts starve and 40 small accounts destroy margin`,
                    diag_smallcut:          `Just cut the 40 small accounts loose and the problem is solved`,
                    diag_topfine:           `Effort is fine — the top 3 accounts deserve the 55% of KAM time they get`,

                    r2_effort_label:        `Reallocate the 6 account managers' time across the segments (must total 100%).`,
