                    rhythm_none:            `No formal rhythm — revisit the portfolio next time something breaks`,
                    under_small:            `The 40 loss-making small accounts — consciously self-serve them; their cost exceeds their value`,
                    under_midtier:          `A high-potential mid-tier account — pull effort back from growth to save cost`,
                    under_none:             `None — we will keep every account at full service; under-serving feels wrong`,

                    warn_effort_value:      `Each segment's KAM time must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_effort_total:      `The 6 account managers' time must total exactly 100%. Re-balance the allocation.`,
                    warn_sow_range:         `Set a share-of-wallet target between 35% (baseline) and 80%.`,
                    warn_conviction_range:  `Conviction must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_overbudget:       `<strong>You have over-committed the €1.5M budget.</strong> It cannot grow this year — every euro past the cap comes out of margin and inflates cost-to-serve. Re-balance the spend before you starve the segments that matter.`,
                    coach_r1_valuepotential: `<strong>That is the right lens.</strong> Value and potential — true profitability and share-of-wallet headroom — not revenue size. It is the only frame that reveals where to invest, where to grow, and where to consciously withdraw.`,
                    coach_r1_margin:        `<strong>Margin beats revenue, but it is only half the picture.</strong> A high-margin account that is expensive to serve can still lose money, and a thin-margin account with huge share-of-wallet headroom can be your best bet. Add potential to the lens.`,
                    coach_r1_revenue:       `<strong>Reading the book by revenue size is Common Error number one.</strong> It makes the €13M anchor look untouchable and hides that 40 small accounts lose €1.1M and 22 mid-tier accounts are starving. Revenue is not profit — re-segment by value and potential.`,
                    coach_r1_skipcts:       `<strong>Without a cost-to-serve view, every effort decision is guesswork.</strong> The 40 unprofitable small accounts look ‘fine’ on a revenue report and quietly destroy margin. Make cost-to-serve visible before you allocate a single account manager.`,
                    coach_r1_topfine:       `<strong>‘The top 3 deserve their 55%’ is exactly the reflex to break.</strong> You are defending revenue you already have while the margin you could win — 22 high-margin mid-tier accounts — starves. Effort is your scarcest resource; where is it actually best spent?`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> Value-and-potential lens plus a full cost-to-serve view — now the 40 margin-destroyers and the 22 starving mid-tier accounts are visible, and your effort allocation next round can be deliberate instead of guesswork.`,
                    coach_r2_anchorheavy:   `<strong>Still pouring effort into the top 3 ‘because that's where the revenue is’.</strong> Your highest-margin mid-tier accounts are worth €9.5M and one just signalled it is leaving. You are defending revenue and ignoring profit. Pull some effort off the anchor.`,
                    coach_r2_midfund:       `<strong>Funding the mid-tier is the growth move.</strong> Those 22 high-margin accounts have the most headroom in the book — proactive management there unlocks margin the top 3 cannot. That is value-and-potential prioritisation in action.`,
                    coach_r2_midstarve:     `<strong>The mid-tier is starving.</strong> 22 high-margin accounts worth €9.5M with real growth headroom, and you have left them on a transactional tier with almost no account-manager time. That is the margin you walked past.`,
                    coach_r2_smalleffort:   `<strong>You are still spending account-manager time on the 40 loss-making small accounts.</strong> They cost more to serve than they generate; high-touch effort there is the leak, not the fix. Move them to self-serve and redeploy the time.`,
                    coach_r2_selfserve:     `<strong>Self-serving the small accounts recovers the €1.1M leak.</strong> A €25k fabricator does not need the same dedicated support as a €13M OEM. Matching service cost to account value is exactly how cost-to-serve management frees capacity for the high-potential mid-tier.`,
                    coach_r2_uniform:       `<strong>One premium package for all 180 accounts is Common Error number two.</strong> You are giving a €25k fabricator the same dedicated support as a €13M OEM and bleeding €1.1M a year. ‘Equal’ service is not fair service — it is unmanaged cost. Tier it.`,
                    coach_r2_overserve:     `<strong>A full strategic-partner tier for the whole mid-tier is over-serving.</strong> Fund proactive growth management, yes — but the most expensive package, the same as the anchor's, inflates cost-to-serve faster than these accounts can repay it this year.`,
                    coach_r3_aisprawl:      `<strong>You switched on all three modules at once — on data that needs €90k of cleanup just to run one well.</strong> You will get three mediocre models and trust none of them. Which single capability most directly serves your portfolio strategy this quarter?`,
                    coach_r3_churn:         `<strong>Churn prediction would have flagged the Aérospatiale risk early.</strong> A defensible choice if retention is your priority this quarter — just make sure it is wired into a real save-play, not just an alert no one acts on.`,
                    coach_r3_scoring:       `<strong>Account scoring re-segments the 180 accounts by value and potential.</strong> It directly powers the effort-allocation discipline you started in Round 1 — the right pick if your core problem is that effort is mis-allocated.`,
                    coach_r3_nba:           `<strong>Next-best-action lifts share-of-wallet via guided cross-sell.</strong> Exactly the lever if your growth thesis is to deepen existing relationships rather than chase new logos. Aim it at the top accounts where the ~35% wallet gap is largest.`,
                    coach_r3_volume:        `<strong>Now the discount buys something.</strong> A targeted cut tied to a multi-year volume commitment, with the engineer sold as a paid service, defends the anchor without giving the margin away for free. That is what a concession should look like.`,
                    coach_r3_value:         `<strong>A value reframe holds your price.</strong> Total cost of ownership, reliability, the engineer as a paid service — you may accept some dual-sourcing, but you protect margin and avoid setting a 9%-discount precedent across the book.`,
                    coach_r3_walkaway:      `<strong>Sometimes the right answer is to let them dual-source 40%.</strong> Keeping the profitable 60% and redeploying that engineer to a growing mid-tier account beats running the whole relationship at a loss. Defending an account has a profitability limit.`,
                    coach_r3_blanket:       `<strong>You matched the 9% and extended it across the book ‘to be consistent’.</strong> That is roughly €2M of gross margin gone — and Aérospatiale may dual-source anyway. You traded margin for a maybe. What did you get in return: a volume commitment, a longer contract, exclusivity — or just a smaller invoice?`,
                    coach_r3_cave:          `<strong>You committed a free dedicated engineer and the full 9% to hold all of Aérospatiale.</strong> That account is now barely profitable after cost-to-serve. Is keeping every box of bolts worth running the relationship at a loss? A no-strings cave is the most expensive way to ‘win’.`,
                    coach_r3_newlogos:      `<strong>Chasing new logos to replace the anchor is Common Error number five.</strong> You hold only ~35% of wallet at your existing top accounts — there is more revenue sitting inside relationships you already have, at a fraction of the acquisition cost. Why hunt cold logos when two-thirds of your customers' spend goes to someone else?`,
                    coach_r3_sharp:         `<strong>That is the integrated play.</strong> One focused AI module, a defence that makes the discount buy a commitment, and growth from share-of-wallet rather than cold logos — the three decisions now reinforce one portfolio strategy.`,
                    coach_r4_crediblesow:   `<strong>A credible, reachable target.</strong> Around 45–55% via cross-sell at your top accounts is a number you can actually hit and defend — far stronger than a heroic figure with no plan behind it.`,
                    coach_r4_overpromise:   `<strong>That share-of-wallet target is a stretch you cannot evidence.</strong> Promising 70%+ without a credible cross-sell plan reads as a slide, not a strategy. Commit to what you can reach and over-deliver.`,
                    coach_r4_rhythm:        `<strong>A quarterly value-and-potential review is what makes the model stick.</strong> Explicit tier rules and built-in AI usage turn a one-off reshuffle into an operating discipline — so effort, service and spend stay matched to value all year.`,
                    coach_r4_norhythm:      `<strong>With no operating rhythm, the portfolio drifts straight back.</strong> Effort creeps back onto the top 3, small accounts re-inflate cost-to-serve, and you are back here next year. Set a cadence and explicit tier rules.`,
                    coach_r4_disciplined:   `<strong>Disciplined to the end.</strong> Consciously self-serving the loss-making small accounts and locking a quarterly review is exactly the KAM discipline — matching effort and service to value, and holding the line on the accounts you chose to under-serve.`,
                    coach_r4_wrongunderserve: `<strong>Under-serving a high-potential mid-tier account is the wrong cut.</strong> That is the growth you wanted to unlock. The accounts that have earned less service are the loss-making small ones — not the mid-tier with the most headroom.`,
                    coach_r4_nounderserve:  `<strong>Refusing to under-serve anyone keeps the leak open.</strong> Consciously under-serving some accounts is a legitimate — often necessary — KAM decision. ‘Equal’ service for all 180 just keeps cost-to-serve bloated and margin destroyed.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
