                    sum_r4_good:            `Credible SoW target, quarterly rhythm, disciplined under-serving.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `No durable rhythm or the wrong account left under-served.`,

                    insight_mastered:       `Portfolio mastered: you read the book by value and potential, made cost-to-serve visible, moved small accounts to self-serve to recover the €1.1M leak, funded the mid-tier, deployed one focused AI module, made the discount buy a commitment, and grew share-of-wallet inside existing relationships. Profit is up on baseline, the anchor held on profitable terms, and the model is locked in a quarterly rhythm.`,
                    insight_bled:           `Margin bled to keep the peace: matching the 9% across the book — or caving to the anchor no-strings — traded roughly €2M of gross margin for a maybe. A discount has to buy a volume commitment, a longer contract or exclusivity. You got a smaller invoice and an account that may still dual-source.`,
                    insight_short:          `A partial recovery. The four KPIs did not all move together — effort, service tiers, the AI choice and the anchor defence still tell slightly different stories. The discipline is matching all of them to value and potential, consistently.`,
                    insight_leak:           `You never moved the 40 loss-making small accounts off the premium tier, so the ≈€1.1M/yr cost-to-serve leak stayed open. ‘Equal’ service is unmanaged cost — self-serve is what recovers that margin.`,
                    insight_sow_strong:     `Share-of-wallet climbed well above the ~35% baseline — you grew inside existing relationships rather than chasing cold logos, the cheaper and more defensible lever in a concentrated B2B book.`,
                    insight_sow_flat:       `Share-of-wallet barely moved off ~35%. With two-thirds of your top accounts' spend going to someone else, that is the single biggest growth opportunity you left untouched — guided cross-sell, not new logos.`,
                    insight_aisprawl:       `You spread budget and immature data across all three AI modules. Three mediocre models you trust none of is worse than one capability deployed well — the SME AI lesson is focus, not breadth.`,
                    insight_anchorover:     `You over-invested to keep 100% of the anchor and it is now barely profitable after cost-to-serve. Defending an account has a profitability limit — sometimes keeping the profitable 60% and redeploying the engineer is the stronger move.`,
                    insight_lean:           `Cost-to-serve fell well below baseline — differentiated service tiers matched cost to value and freed real capacity for the high-potential accounts. That is cost-to-serve management working as intended.`,

                    hint_blanket:           `The blanket 9% discount vaporised margin for no commitment. A concession has to buy something — re-anchor on a volume-tied or value-based defence.`,
                    hint_aisprawl:          `You deployed all three AI modules on immature data. Focus on the single capability that serves your portfolio strategy this quarter.`,
                    hint_leak:              `The 40 small accounts are still on a premium tier, leaking ≈€1.1M/yr. Move them to self-serve and match service cost to account value.`,
                    hint_cts:               `Cost-to-serve is bloated. Differentiate your tiers: self-serve the small accounts and stop over-serving the mid-tier with the anchor's package.`,
                    hint_sow:               `Share-of-wallet is stuck near the ~35% baseline. Grow inside existing top accounts via cross-sell before chasing new logos.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — profit up, anchor held and share-of-wallet growing. Lock in a quarterly rhythm and hold the line on the accounts you chose to under-serve.`,

